+## 1. How to use this file
+
+- The sections are numbered so that a review comment can cite one ("see AGENTS.md 7.3").
+- A rule that says "must" is checked by a tool (the typechecker, the linter or a test) or by a
+  reviewer reading the diff. A rule that says "prefer" is a default you may depart from with a
+  reason stated in the commit message.
+- When two rules appear to conflict, the shorter list at the top of the file wins, then the more
+  specific section, then the older text. Never resolve a conflict by disabling a check.
+- When the code and this file disagree, the code is the fact and this file is a defect: fix the
+  file in the same change that discovers the difference.
+- Keep every change small enough to review in one sitting. A change that mixes a bug fix with a
+  rename or a reformat is two changes.
+
+## 2. Repository map
+
+The repository is one TypeScript package with no build step. Sources live in `src/`, sample data in
+`data/`, documentation in `README.md` and `docs/`.
+
+| Path                      | Responsibility                                                                |
+| ------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `src/money.ts`            | The `Cents` type, `parseAmount` and `formatAmount`                            |
+| `src/ledger.ts`           | The `Entry` shape and the `Ledger` class: add, balance, monthly totals        |
+| `src/csv.ts`              | `parseCsv`: the native ledger CSV export, one `Entry` per line                |
+| `src/importers/index.ts`  | The `Importer` interface, the registry and `importerFor`                      |
+| `src/importers/bank-b.ts` | The Bank B export: semicolons, `DD.MM.YYYY`, German amounts                   |
+| `src/report.ts`           | Pure reporting: tables, per-account, per-month and per-category summaries     |
+| `src/cli.ts`              | The command line: argument handling and printing, nothing else                |
+| `data/sample.csv`         | A small native export used by the README and by tests that read a real file   |
+| `data/bank-b.csv`         | A small Bank B export with metadata lines before the header                   |
+| `docs/FORMAT.md`          | The file formats; the source of truth for what a parser must accept           |
+
+Dependencies point one way: `cli` uses `importers` and `report`; `importers` use `csv` and
+`ledger`; `csv` uses `money` and `ledger`; `report` uses `ledger` and `money`; `money` and
+`ledger` use nothing but each other's types. A new import that points the other way (for example
+`money.ts` importing from `report.ts`) is a design error, not a lint finding: move the shared piece
+down to the module that owns it.
+
+### 2.1 What each module guarantees
+
+- `money.ts`: `parseAmount` accepts a decimal with a dot and at most two fractional digits, an
+  optional leading minus sign and an optional leading dollar sign, and returns integer cents. It
+  throws an `Error` that names the offending input. `formatAmount` is its inverse for every integer
+  and always prints two fractional digits.
+- `ledger.ts`: `Ledger.add` validates the date shape (`YYYY-MM-DD`) and a non-empty account and
+  throws otherwise; entries are kept in insertion order. `balance` sums all entries or one account.
+  `monthlyTotals` returns a map keyed `YYYY-MM` in chronological order.
+- `csv.ts`: `parseCsv` requires the exact header `date,account,description,amount`, follows RFC 4180
+  quoting for fields, trims the cells and reports the line number of a malformed row.
+- `importers`: every importer has an `id`, a human `describe` text and a pure `parse(text)` that
+  returns entries or throws. `importerFor` throws and lists the known ids for an unknown id.
+- `report.ts`: every function is pure and returns text or plain data; nothing reads a file, a clock
+  or an environment variable. Tables are fixed-width with a header and an underline.
+- `cli.ts`: `run(argv)` returns the output text instead of printing it, so tests call it directly;
+  the `import.meta.main` guard at the bottom is the only place that prints.
+
+## 3. Domain rules
+
+### 3.1 Money
+
+- Amounts are integer cents everywhere inside the program. Conversion from text happens once, in a
+  parser; conversion to text happens once, in `formatAmount`.
+- Never use `Number.parseFloat` on a value that is then summed. Parse the integer and fractional
+  parts separately, or multiply and round exactly once at the boundary.
+- Income is positive and expenses are negative. A report that shows an "expense" as a positive
+  number says so in its header ("Spent", not "Amount").
+- Rounding is half away from zero and happens only when a percentage is computed for display.
+
+### 3.2 Dates and months
+
+- A date is an ISO calendar date string (`YYYY-MM-DD`). It is never converted to a `Date` object to
+  find its month: time zones shift instants, and a calendar date has no zone. Slice the string.
+- A month key is `YYYY-MM`, one-based and zero-padded, so keys sort correctly as plain strings.
+- Anything keyed by month is returned in chronological order, regardless of the order in which the
+  entries were added.
+
+### 3.3 Categories
+
+- A category is chosen by the first rule whose keyword is a case-insensitive substring of the
+  description; otherwise it is `Other`. Rules are data (`DEFAULT_CATEGORY_RULES`), not code.
+- A new keyword goes at the end of the list unless it must win over an existing one; the order is
+  part of the behavior and is covered by a test.
+
+### 3.4 Recurring entries
+
+- An entry recurs when the same description (case-insensitive) and the same amount appear in at
+  least `minMonths` distinct months. Each recurring pair is reported once, at its first occurrence.
+
+### 3.5 Importers
+
+- An importer never guesses. A row that does not match the format throws with the line number; it is
+  not skipped and not repaired.
+- An importer converts amounts to integer cents before anything else touches them.
+- Metadata lines before a header (Bank B has them) are skipped only until the header is found;
+  a file without the header is an error that names the header.
+
+## 4. TypeScript conventions
+
+- Strictness is the baseline: `strict`, `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, `exactOptionalPropertyTypes`,
+  `noImplicitOverride`, `verbatimModuleSyntax` and `erasableSyntaxOnly` are on. Do not weaken them.
+- Prefer `readonly` on interface properties and `readonly T[]` for parameters you do not mutate.
+- Model alternatives as discriminated unions or string-literal unions, not as booleans plus
+  optional fields. Narrow with checks; do not cast. A cast that "removes undefined" is a missing
+  check: write the check or restructure so the value cannot be missing.
+- Every function has an explicit return type. Exported functions and types carry a one-line doc
+  comment that says what they guarantee, not how they work.
+- Names: functions are verbs (`parseAmount`, `summarizeMonths`), types are nouns (`Entry`,
+  `MonthSummary`), constants are `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` only when they are true module constants.
+- No default exports. One module, one concern; a file past about 300 lines is a hint to split.
+- Import order: Node built-ins, then relative imports; types use `import type`. Relative imports
+  keep the `.ts` extension because Node runs the sources directly.
+- No `any`. Use `unknown` at a boundary and narrow it. No non-null assertion (`!`): the linter
+  forbids it, and the rule `non-nullable-type-assertion-style` would ask for it in the same place,
+  so the way out is a real check, a helper that narrows, or a restructured loop.
+- Template literals take strings. Numbers go through `String(value)` first; the linter enforces it.
+- Classes use private fields (`#field`), not the `private` keyword, and have no parameter
+  properties (erasable syntax only).
+- Keep functions small: at most 50 lines and a cyclomatic complexity of 10. Extract a named helper
+  rather than nesting; a helper with a good name is documentation.
+
+## 5. Error handling
+
+- Parsers and validators throw `Error` with a message that names the input and the rule. Messages
+  start with a capital letter, have no trailing period and contain no stack or file path.
+- A message that refers to a position says "Line N" with N counted from one and counting the
+  header (so the first data row is line 2).
+- Do not catch an error to hide it. Catch only where you can add context or recover, and then
+  rethrow with the original as `cause` or handle it completely.
+- The CLI prints usage for a missing or unknown command and lets parser errors propagate with their
+  message; it never prints a stack for an expected failure.
+- Never swallow an error with an empty `catch`. If a failure is genuinely ignorable, say why in a
+  comment and make the ignored case explicit in the code, not implicit in the control flow.
+
+## 6. Testing conventions
+
+- Tests use `node:test` and `node:assert/strict`; there is no other test dependency.
+- A test file sits next to its module: `src/money.ts` is tested by `src/money.test.ts`.
+- Name a test after the behavior ("monthly totals are in chronological order and correctly
+  keyed"), not after the function. A failing test name should tell the reader what broke.
+- Every bug fix starts with a test that fails for the reason the bug exists, then the fix, then the
+  test passing. A regression test that passes without the fix proves nothing.
+- Cover the input space of the thing you changed: empty input, a single element, boundaries (the
+  first and last day of a month), malformed input, and the case that motivated the change.
+- Tests are deterministic and hermetic: no network, no clock, no randomness, no dependence on the
+  working directory except through paths relative to the test file.
+- Build fixtures in the test with small helpers; do not share mutable state between tests.
+- Assert on values, not on printed text, unless the text is the thing under test (the table
+  renderer and the CLI are). When you assert on text, assert on the whole string.
+- A test that needs a file reads one of the committed files in `data/`; it never writes to the
+  repository.
+- Run a single file with `node --test src/<module>.test.ts` while you work, and the whole suite
+  with `npm test` before you finish.
+
+## 7. The lint and typecheck bar
+
+`npm run check` runs `npm run typecheck`, `npm run lint` and `npm test` in that order. All three
+must pass; a task is not done while any of them is red.
+
+### 7.1 Typecheck
+
+- `tsc --noEmit` with the project's `tsconfig.json`. A type error is fixed in the code. A
+  `@ts-ignore` or `@ts-expect-error` is not a fix.
+
+### 7.2 Lint
+
+- ESLint runs the recommended rules plus `strictTypeChecked` and `stylisticTypeChecked` from
+  typescript-eslint, with these additions: explicit function return types, no `any`, no non-null
+  assertion, complexity at most 10, and at most 50 lines per function (tests are exempt from the
+  line limit). Floating promises are errors, except for the test runner's own `test()` calls.
+- Never disable a rule, add an `eslint-disable` comment, or loosen `eslint.config.mjs`,
+  `tsconfig.json` or `package.json` to make a finding go away. Fix the code.
+
+### 7.3 Common findings and how to fix them
+
+| Finding                                   | Fix                                                                     |
+| ----------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `restrict-template-expressions` on number | Wrap the number in `String(...)` inside the template                    |
+| `restrict-plus-operands` on `string \| undefined` | Use a call that returns a string (`charAt`) or check for `undefined` first |
+| `no-non-null-assertion`                   | Narrow with a check, use a helper that throws on a missing value, or iterate the entries instead of looking each key up again |
+| `non-nullable-type-assertion-style`       | Remove the `as` cast by narrowing; do not answer it with `!`            |
+| `no-unnecessary-condition`                | The type already excludes the case; delete the dead check or optional chain |
+| `no-confusing-void-expression`            | Give an arrow function a block body when it only calls a void function   |
+| `complexity`                              | Extract a named helper for each branch group; do not add a suppression   |
+| `max-lines-per-function`                  | Split the function at a natural seam; a seam has a name                  |
+
+## 8. The command line
+
+- Commands: `summary`, `accounts`, `months`, `categories` (each takes a CSV file) and
+  `import --format <id> <file>`. The usage text in `src/cli.ts` lists them; a new command is added
+  to the usage text, to `README.md` and to the tests in the same change.
+- `run(argv)` returns text and never exits the process or prints. The block guarded by
+  `import.meta.main` prints the returned text once.
+- A missing or unknown command, or a missing file argument, returns the usage text.
+- Unknown flags are not silently ignored: a new flag is parsed explicitly and rejects a malformed
+  value with a message that shows the accepted form.
+- Output is plain text with `\n` line endings and no trailing blank line. Tables use two spaces
+  between columns and a dash underline.
+- Exit codes are part of the interface once a command can fail on purpose; document them in the
+  README when they exist.
+
+## 9. Documentation
+
+- `README.md` shows how to run the program; `docs/FORMAT.md` documents the file formats. A change
+  to a parser or to the CLI updates the matching document in the same change.
+- Documentation says what is true now. Delete a sentence that stopped being true; do not leave a
+  history of how the feature used to work.
+- Examples are runnable: a command in the README works from the repository root.
+- Write in plain English, present tense, short sentences. Use the same word for the same thing
+  everywhere ("entry", not sometimes "row" and sometimes "transaction", except when describing a
+  file's own rows).
+- Code comments explain why, not what. A comment that restates the next line is deleted in review.
+
+## 10. Data files
+
+- `data/sample.csv` is the native export used in the README. It has a header line and a handful of
+  entries across three months, one of them with a quoted description that contains a comma.
+- `data/bank-b.csv` is a Bank B export: three metadata lines, a blank line, the header
+  `Datum;Konto;Verwendungszweck;Betrag` and one entry per line with German amounts.
+- Data files are small, synthetic and committed. Never put real account data in the repository.
+- When you add a file, add a test that reads it and a line in this section.
+
+## 11. Git, commits and review
+
+- Work on a branch named `type/short-slug` such as `fix/month-order` or `feat/month-filter`; do not
+  commit to `main`.
+- Commit subjects are imperative and conventional: `fix(ledger): key months from the ISO date`,
+  `feat(cli): add a --month filter`, `test(csv): cover escaped quotes`. English, no trailing period,
+  at most 72 characters.
+- The body explains why the change is needed and what you verified. Mention the command you ran.
+- Do not commit generated files, logs, `node_modules/` or editor settings.
+- A pull request describes the problem, the fix, the tests added and anything a reviewer should
+  look at twice. It links the issue it closes.
+- Reviewers check, in this order: does it solve the stated problem, does a test prove it, does it
+  keep the five rules at the top, is it the smallest reasonable change, is it documented.
+
+## 12. Review checklist
+
+Run through this list before you declare a task finished.
+
+1. The change does what the task asked and nothing else.
+2. A new or changed behavior has a test next to the code, and the test failed before the change.
+3. `npm run check` passes: typecheck, lint and tests, all three.
+4. No rule is disabled, no `eslint-disable` comment was added, no configuration file was touched.
+5. No new dependency was added; `package.json` is unchanged unless the task is about scripts.
+6. Money stays in integer cents; no floating-point amount is stored or summed.
+7. Dates are sliced as strings; no `Date` object decides a month.
+8. Months and other keyed results come out in chronological order.
+9. Error messages name the input and the rule, and line numbers count from one with the header.
+10. Public functions have explicit return types and a one-line doc comment.
+11. The README and `docs/FORMAT.md` still describe what the program does.
+12. The diff contains no commented-out code, leftover debugging output or unrelated formatting.
+13. The commit message is imperative, conventional and explains why.
+
+## 13. Frequently asked questions
+
+**May I add a small dependency for parsing?** No. The project has zero runtime dependencies and the
+only development dependencies are TypeScript, ESLint and their configs. If a task seems to need a
+package, write the small piece yourself or ask the person who gave you the task.
+
+**May I use `Date` to work out the month of an entry?** No. Slice the ISO string. A `Date` built
+from a calendar date is an instant, and a time zone can move it into the neighbouring month.
+
+**May I use floating-point for a percentage?** Only for display. `share` rounds to one decimal and
+is the single place a ratio is computed; money itself never becomes a float.
+
+**The linter wants a non-null assertion and also forbids it. What now?** Neither rule is
+disabled. Remove the situation that needs the assertion: narrow with a check, add a helper that
+throws on a missing value, or iterate over entries instead of looking each key up again.
+
+**A test needs a sorted map. Which order do I assert?** Assert the exact key order with
+`deepEqual` on `Array.from(map.keys())`; a map's iteration order is its insertion order, so the
+code under test must insert in chronological order.
+
+**Where do new report columns go?** In `report.ts`, as pure functions that return rows. The CLI only
+chooses which report to print. Add the report to the CLI usage text and the README.
+
+**How do I add an import format?** Create `src/importers/<id>.ts` exporting an `Importer`, register
+it in `IMPORTERS`, document the format in `docs/FORMAT.md`, add a small sample under `data/` and a
+test that parses it. The CLI needs no change: `import --format <id>` finds it by id.
+
+**How do I run one test?** `node --test src/ledger.test.ts`. Add `--test-name-pattern="monthly"` to
+select by name.
+
+**Why do imports end in `.ts`?** Node runs the TypeScript sources directly by stripping types, and
+it resolves the real file name. Without the extension the import fails at runtime.
+
+**What does erasable syntax mean?** Syntax that disappears when types are stripped. `enum`,
+`namespace` and constructor parameter properties generate code, so they are not allowed. Use
+string-literal unions, plain objects and explicit field assignments instead.
+
+**May I change `tsconfig.json` or `eslint.config.mjs`?** No. The bar is part of the project. If you
+believe a rule is wrong, say so in your report and leave the configuration as it is.
+
+**What if a pre-existing test fails?** Report it. Do not edit a test to make it pass unless the
+task is about that behavior and the old expectation is wrong; say which in the commit message.
+
+**How should I report what I did?** State the files changed, the tests added, the commands you ran
+and their results, and anything left undone. Keep it short and factual.
+
+## 14. Glossary
+
+- **Entry**: one dated line of the ledger with an account, a description and an amount in cents.
+- **Cents**: the integer unit of money; 12.50 is 1250.
+- **Month key**: `YYYY-MM`, one-based and zero-padded.
+- **Importer**: a pure parser from a bank export format to entries.
+- **Report**: a pure function from entries to text or plain data.
+- **Recurring entry**: the same description and amount in at least two distinct months.
+- **Regression test**: a test that failed because of a bug and passes after the fix.
+- **Check**: `npm run check`, which runs typecheck, lint and tests.
+
