+## 17. Worked examples
+
+### 17.1 Fixing a parser bug
+
+1. Reproduce it with the smallest input that fails, as a test next to the parser.
+2. Run the test and confirm that it fails for the reason you expect and not because of a typo.
+3. Fix the parser at the point where the wrong value is produced, not where it is noticed.
+4. Add the neighbouring cases: an empty field, a quoted field, a last line without a newline.
+5. Run `npm run check` and fix every lint finding in the code.
+6. Update `docs/FORMAT.md` when the accepted format changed.
+
+### 17.2 Adding a report
+
+1. Write the pure function in `src/report.ts` that returns plain data: a summary type with
+   `readonly` fields and no formatting.
+2. Write the renderer that turns that data into text with `renderTable`.
+3. Test the data function with a small fixed list of entries and the renderer against the whole
+   expected text.
+4. Register the command in `REPORTS` and add it to the usage text in `src/cli.ts`.
+5. Add a test that calls `run([...])` with a file from `data/`.
+6. Document the command in `README.md`.
+
+### 17.3 Adding a command-line option
+
+1. Decide the accepted form and the error message for a malformed value before writing code.
+2. Parse the option explicitly in `run`; nothing else reads `process.argv`.
+3. Apply it as a filter on the entries before a report is built, so every report benefits.
+4. Add tests for the option present, absent and malformed.
+5. Add the option to the usage text and to the README.
+
+### 17.4 A regression test, as a template
+
+```ts
+import assert from "node:assert/strict";
+import { test } from "node:test";
+import { Ledger } from "./ledger.ts";
+
+test("monthly totals are in chronological order and correctly keyed", () => {
+  const ledger = new Ledger();
+  ledger.add({ date: "2026-02-01", account: "a", description: "Feb", amount: 100 });
+  ledger.add({ date: "2026-01-01", account: "a", description: "Jan", amount: 200 });
+  assert.deepEqual(Array.from(ledger.monthlyTotals().keys()), ["2026-01", "2026-02"]);
+});
+```
+
+## 18. Troubleshooting
+
+**`npm run lint` reports a finding in a test file.** Tests are linted with the same rules as the
+sources, except the function-length limit. Fix the test; do not exclude it.
+
+**`node --test` finds no tests.** Run it from the repository root. Test files end in `.test.ts`
+and live next to their module under `src/`.
+
+**Node reports `ERR_UNSUPPORTED_TYPESCRIPT_SYNTAX`.** The file uses syntax that type stripping
+cannot erase: an `enum`, a `namespace` or a constructor parameter property. Replace it with a
+string-literal union, a plain object or an explicit field assignment.
+
+**Node reports `Cannot find module './ledger'`.** A relative import is missing its `.ts` extension.
+Node resolves the real file name; write `./ledger.ts`.
+
+**A map comes out in the wrong order.** Maps iterate in insertion order. Sort the keys (they are
+`YYYY-MM` strings, so a plain string comparison is chronological) and build the result map from
+the sorted keys, never from the insertion order of the entries.
+
+**A total is off by one cent.** A floating-point value was summed somewhere. Find where the amount
+was parsed, make sure it becomes integer cents there, and add a test with an amount such as `0.10`
+added three times.
+
+**The typechecker says an array element may be `undefined`.** `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` is on, so
+an indexed read is `T | undefined`. Check it, destructure with a default, or iterate with `for...of`
+instead of indexing. Do not answer it with a cast or a non-null assertion.
+
+**A test passes on your machine and fails in review.** It depends on the time zone, the clock, the
+working directory or the order of a map. Make the dependency explicit in the test or remove it.
+
+## 19. More questions
+
+**Should a new helper be exported?** Only if another module or a test needs it. Everything else
+stays module-private; an exported name is a promise.
+
+**Where does shared formatting live?** Money formatting lives in `money.ts`, table formatting in
+`report.ts`. A third place for formatting is a sign that one of those two should grow instead.
+
+**May a report read the current date?** No. Reports are pure: they take entries and return text.
+A feature that needs "today" receives it as a parameter from the CLI.
+
+**Can I rename a public function?** Yes, in one change that updates every caller, the tests and the
+documentation. Leave no alias behind.
+
+**How long may a test be?** As long as it needs to be to state one behavior. If a test has two
+unrelated assertions, it is two tests.
+
+**What goes in the commit body?** Why the change was needed, what you verified and how. The diff
+already says what changed.
+
+**What if the task text and this file disagree?** The task text says what to do; this file says how
+the project works. If following the task would break one of the five rules at the top, stop and
+say so instead of breaking the rule.
+
+**Is a TODO comment acceptable?** Only with a reference to an open issue, and never to hide an
+unfinished branch. Prefer finishing the work or deleting the code path.
+
+## 20. Style notes
+
+- Formatting is consistent within a file: match the quotes, the trailing commas and the line width
+  of the code around your change. There is no formatter to run, so read the neighbouring lines.
+- Prefer early returns over nested conditionals, and small named helpers over long functions.
+- Prefer `for...of` over index loops unless you need the index; prefer `Map` and `Set` over plain
+  objects used as dictionaries.
+- Prefer returning a new value over mutating an argument. A function that must mutate says so in
+  its name or its doc comment.
+- Keep comments in English and keep them true. Delete a comment instead of updating it when the
+  code became obvious.
+- Choose the plainest construct that is correct. A reader should never need to wonder why a clever
+  expression was used instead of two simple statements.
+- When you are unsure how a module is meant to behave, read its test file first: the tests are the
+  most precise statement of the intended behavior in this repository.
